---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/holiday-gifting';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/holiday-gifting');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/holiday-gifting');
const candidates = ["sequenzy", "klaviyo", "omnisend", "privy", "yotpo-email-sms", "drip", "shopify-email", "postscript", "mailchimp", "aftership-email"].map(s => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
<a href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
<h1>Run holiday gifting around promises you can fulfill</h1>
<p>A gift campaign has two identities to respect: the person paying and the person receiving. It also has a delivery promise that changes by product, destination and service. This proposed operating plan helps a Shopify merchant prepare those decisions. The examples are exercises, not performed merchant tests or reported revenue results.</p>
<h2>Collect gift intent without inventing the recipient</h2>
<p>Use an explicit gift option, a reliable order attribute or a preference the shopper supplied. A December purchase or a different shipping address is not conclusive gift intent. Keep an unknown branch for orders without a dependable signal. Document where the property lives and whether it is available in the messaging system before designing branches around it.</p>
<p>A buyer's marketing permission does not transfer to a recipient. Do not add a shipping recipient to promotional sequences merely because an order contains their address. Separate buyer receipts, recipient delivery communication and marketing eligibility. Avoid sending product usage or replenishment assumptions to someone who purchased for another person.</p>
<h2>Build a cutoff register with an owner</h2>
<p>For each promoted product group, record destination, processing time, carrier service, personalization lead time and the latest approved order time with timezone. Give operations ownership of the promise and marketing ownership of the message. Include exceptions for remote regions, made-to-order items and bundles. A carrier estimate alone does not account for warehouse processing.</p>
<p>Distinguish a planned dispatch date from an estimated arrival date and from a guarantee. Use the wording operations can support. Put an expiry on campaign approval so a delayed send cannot reuse an obsolete promise. If disruption changes the cutoff, identify queued messages, landing pages and automations that need revision or cancellation.</p>
<h2>Sequence by actual milestones</h2>
<p>Start with inspiration while there is time for consideration. Follow with curated choices, then the relevant physical-delivery cutoff. A final digital-gift message is appropriate only where that product is available and its delivery behavior has been checked. Dates such as four weeks out or three days out are planning placeholders; derive the real calendar from fulfillment constraints.</p>
<p>Keep destinations and service options separate when their deadlines differ. Recheck eligibility immediately before dispatch. Someone who purchased after entering the sequence should not receive a misleading final warning to buy the same gift. Decide whether they should exit, receive complementary information or remain eligible for a different gift need.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: perishable food gifts</h2>
<p>Choose one cold-chain or shelf-life-sensitive gift and map the order, packing, dispatch and arrival windows. Test a local address, a distant destination and a destination you do not serve. The email must not advertise the broadest delivery promise when only the local route qualifies. Check inventory for every bundle component before promoting a set.</p>
<p>Prepare an alternate message for a delayed dispatch or a sold-out ingredient. A replacement product needs its own approval, especially where allergens or storage instructions differ. Success in this rehearsal means the promise and eligibility are correct; it does not establish higher conversion or lower returns.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: considered jewelry gifts</h2>
<p>Separate ready-to-ship pieces from engraving, sizing or custom work. An appointment invitation can be more useful than deadline pressure for custom items. Put the applicable lead time near the action, and link to the exchange and sizing policy. Do not imply that a standard shipping cutoff covers personalization time.</p>
<p>For gift-buyer follow-up, explain gift receipts and the actual return window. If a recipient needs sizing help, provide a route that does not expose the buyer's private purchase history unnecessarily. A template should not promise an extended return policy unless the merchant has adopted it for those products and dates.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: home goods and bundles</h2>
<p>Build a guide by recipient need or room rather than placing every sale product in a grid. Check dimensions, delivery restrictions and whether bundle components ship together. If an item is unavailable, decide whether the guide hides it, offers a clearly described alternative or stops the send. Do not let a stale image imply that a complete bundle is available.</p>
<p>A popup can ask which collection interests the shopper, but preference capture and sequence execution are separate requirements. Demonstrate that a changed preference reaches the sending system and that an unknown answer gets useful general content. Avoid claiming that a quiz integration or gift branch exists in every candidate app.</p>
<h2>Digital gifts are a separate operational path</h2>
<p>Verify who receives the digital gift, whether delivery is immediate or scheduled, and how an incorrect address is corrected. Test the customer-facing redemption instructions and the support route for missing delivery. Do not substitute a digital gift automatically for an unfulfillable physical order. The buyer should understand the product and its restrictions before purchase.</p>
<p>Keep physical-item cutoff messaging out of digital-only follow-up. Conversely, do not advertise instant delivery based solely on a template label. Check the actual product configuration and order handling used by your store.</p>
<h2>Corporate gift buyers need a different handoff</h2>
<p>Bulk buyers may need invoicing, multiple destinations, personalization approval and a sales contact. Route these requests to an owner who can confirm capacity before quoting deadlines. A consumer coupon and a last-day warning are poor substitutes for an agreed production schedule. Last year's bulk purchase should prompt a relevance check rather than an assumption of current intent.</p>
<p>Capture requested quantity and delivery window without promising acceptance. Keep corporate orders out of consumer urgency messages while a quote is being negotiated. Define how the sales owner marks a request complete or no longer active so suppression does not last indefinitely.</p>
<h2>Coordinate email and optional SMS</h2>
<p>Email can carry curation, delivery qualifications and return details. A short text may draw attention to a relevant deadline for eligible subscribers, but it still needs a truthful destination and current eligibility. Nominate one campaign owner to prevent contradictory dates or repeated urgency across systems. Do not assume that two products share suppression automatically.</p>
<p>Review destination eligibility, consent records, quiet-hour settings and opt-outs through your chosen provider before enabling SMS. Budget for each proposed send and the incentive, if any. Channel expansion is a decision to validate, not evidence that every listed email product can execute SMS.</p>
<h2>Measure the season without overstating attribution</h2>
<p>Track eligible recipients, purchases, contribution after gift packaging and service costs, deadline-related support contacts and refunds. Separate known gift, known self-purchase and unknown groups. Review physical goods and digital gifts separately. A large share of December attributed revenue does not show that the campaign caused those purchases.</p>
<p>After returns have matured, review whether the guide set the right expectations. Keep the actual cutoff decisions, disruptions and abandoned promises for next year's planning. Do not simply reuse last year's carrier dates. January follow-up can invite an eligible buyer to shop for themselves, but it should not assume the recipient's usage or replenishment needs.</p>
<h2>Choose tooling by demonstration</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/features/shopify-integration">Sequenzy describes a Shopify integration</a>; this alone is not proof of a ready-made gift-order branch, deadline engine or automatic gift-card pivot. Require a demonstration with the exact order properties and dispatch rechecks you plan to use. The same standard applies to every candidate below.</p>
<p><a href="/use-cases/email-design">Email design</a> owns readable product information and missing-data layouts. <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Discount strategy</a> owns code combinations and the contribution floor. This gifting workflow owns buyer/recipient identity, fulfillment promises and the seasonal handoff. Use <a href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase</a> planning for support after the order rather than adding every buyer to an unrelated tutorial series.</p>
<h2>App discovery without a feature verdict</h2>
<p>These are research starting points, not a tested ranking. Ask suppliers to demonstrate your required data, permissions, template behavior and failure cases in the plan you would buy. Confirm current pricing directly; an email editor does not establish SMS support or a shared suppression system.</p>
<ul>{candidates.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
